WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.013
<v Instructor>So now let's talk</v>

2
00:00:01.013 --> 00:00:03.840
about service control policies, or SCP.

3
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.180
So here, you can define allowlist or blocklist

4
00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.240
for specific IAM actions on your organization

5
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.680
for your accounts.

6
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.350
And this applies either at the OU level

7
00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.030
or the Account level.

8
00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:18.300
Any SCP you apply to your management accounts

9
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.800
actually is disregarded

10
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.230
because you want to keep your management account safe

11
00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.720
from any kind of lockout.

12
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.450
The SCPs is going to be applied to all the users

13
00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:30.480
and the roles in the account, including the root user.

14
00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.450
And the SCP does not affect any service-linked roles.

15
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.030
That means that these services, those service-linked role,

16
00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.130
they enable other services on AWS

17
00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.200
to integrate with organization,

18
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.320
and therefore that's why they can't be restricted by SCPs.

19
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.240
So, SCPs are special.

20
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.780
And for something to be allowed within organizations,

21
00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.360
you need to make sure that every SCP along the way

22
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.850
from the root to each OU down

23
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:57.390
to the specific target account will have an explicit allow.

24
00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.420
And I will show you this in the next slide as an example.

25
00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:01.920
So the use cases for SCP is

26
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.780
to restrict access to certain services.

27
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.360
For example, hey, you cannot use EMR,

28
00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.400
or to enforce PCI compliance

29
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.570
by explicitly disabling some services in AWS.

30
00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.150
This will be very clear in this example.

31
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.163
So let's take for example, a Root OU,

32
00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:21.510
and we apply FullAWSAccess underneath.

33
00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.850
So the management account is under the Root OU,

34
00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.460
and then we have more OU, the Sandbox, the Workload,

35
00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.820
Test, and Prod, with accounts within the OUs.

36
00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.130
So if we apply an SCP,

37
00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:35.610
which is Deny Athena on the management account,

38
00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:37.500
what is happening to the management account?

39
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.690
Well, the management account can do anything

40
00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:43.140
because there's a rule that says that no SCP ever apply

41
00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.550
to the management account.

42
00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.190
This is a safety because of course if you lock yourself out

43
00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:49.710
of your management account, this would be terrible.

44
00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.530
So remember, the management accounts can do anything

45
00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:53.880
no matter what.

46
00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:54.840
But let's say for example,

47
00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.210
we apply on the Sandbox OU

48
00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.750
a FullAWSAccess plus Deny S3.

49
00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:02.760
So as you can see here, we still need

50
00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.670
to apply FullAWSAccess to apply

51
00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.340
to give allow to everything explicitly

52
00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:11.190
because if we didn't have full AWS access,

53
00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.470
then nothing would be allowed within that OU.

54
00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:16.380
So explicit allows are needed every single way.

55
00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.000
And on account A, say we also do apply

56
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.057
FullAWSAccess and Deny EC2.

57
00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.990
Then account A can do anything

58
00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:28.560
because it has full AWS access on the Root OU

59
00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.890
and on the Sandbox OU and on the account itself.

60
00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:33.960
But because we have two explicit denies,

61
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.630
we have one Deny S3 from the Sandbox

62
00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:40.350
and one Deny EC2 from the account itself,

63
00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:43.533
then it cannot access Amazon S3 or Amazon EC2.

64
00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:48.300
For Account B and C, because they don't have specific SCP

65
00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.520
attached to them, then they can do anything

66
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.830
except accessing Amazon S3

67
00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.080
because you have an explicit deny coming

68
00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.003
from the Sandbox OU.

69
00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.790
Next, if we apply FullAWSAccess to the Workloads OU,

70
00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:07.350
and we also apply an Allow EC2 SCP to the Test OU,

71
00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:10.590
then Account D can only access EC2.

72
00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.540
And if we look at the Prod OU

73
00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.710
and Account E and F within it, well, we must first apply

74
00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:19.980
the FullAWSAccess SCP to the Prod OU,

75
00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:23.310
and then Account E and F can do anything.

76
00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.160
So here are some examples for SCPs.

77
00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.940
This is the first one is a blocking of a specific service.

78
00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.350
So first we allow all actions, and then we DenyDynamoDB,

79
00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:39.240
or on the allow list here, we allow EC2 and CloudWatch,

80
00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.920
but we don't allow anything else.

81
00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.740
So those are some examples of SCPs.

82
00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:47.220
So let's have a look at the IAM policy evaluation logic,

83
00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.920
because this diagram basically explains to you

84
00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.920
how you are authorized or not to do actions within AWS.

85
00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:56.070
And you don't need to know it by heart,

86
00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.930
but it should make sense to you.

87
00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:59.790
So we can see there's a whole flow,

88
00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.670
but at every step there is an evaluation.

89
00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:05.370
And so we're going to look at Deny evaluation.

90
00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:07.530
We're going to look at Organizations SCP,

91
00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.810
Resource-based policy, Identity-based policy,

92
00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:10.830
IAM permission boundaries,

93
00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:12.990
and then finally, Session policies

94
00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:16.920
to allow or deny a specific IAM action.

95
00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.870
So let's have a look in details to see how that works.

96
00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:22.470
So, we're going to look at all the possible policies

97
00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.570
in here, and if there's an explicit deny,

98
00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:27.570
then we know that it's going to be denied automatically.

99
00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:30.240
Then we look at the organization's SCP,

100
00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.070
and is there an allow?

101
00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:33.510
If yes, you go to next step.

102
00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:38.040
If not, then it's a deny because it's an implicit deny.

103
00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.290
Then we look at resource-based policy, for example,

104
00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:42.570
they can be applied to S3 buckets.

105
00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:44.700
They can be applied to SQS SQs and so on.

106
00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:45.533
And again, we have a look.

107
00:04:45.533 --> 00:04:47.040
Is there a resource-based policy?

108
00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.170
If so, is there an allow?

109
00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:51.150
Yes or no?

110
00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:52.920
Then we have identity-based policy.

111
00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:54.540
So we look again at whether

112
00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.040
or not there's an identity-based policy,

113
00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.540
whether or not there's an allow.

114
00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:01.620
And then we deny implicitly or go on to the next step.

115
00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.380
Then we look at IAM permission boundaries that we just saw.

116
00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:06.180
And then finally we look at session policies.

117
00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:08.880
I won't go over them, this is more related to STS.

118
00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.320
But what I want you to remember is

119
00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.960
that all these things are evaluated

120
00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.710
when making a specific IAM action, okay?

121
00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:20.130
And so only if all these things are allowing

122
00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:22.950
and working together or not denying specifically,

123
00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.080
then you will have a finalization allow,

124
00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.840
and you'll be able to do your action, okay?

125
00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:29.640
So it's just to give you a bit more overview,

126
00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:30.900
but hopefully you understand better

127
00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:33.540
how security works in AWS.

128
00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.600
So these IAM SCP policies can be used, for example,

129
00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.610
to restrict tags, okay?

130
00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:42.030
So, if you want to restrict specific tags on AWS resources,

131
00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:45.690
you can use the AWS TagKeys Condition Key.

132
00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:48.720
Now, this will allow you to validate the tag keys

133
00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.030
attached to your resource against the tag keys

134
00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:52.110
in the IAM policy.

135
00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:53.730
And this is something that comes up in the exam.

136
00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:57.450
So if we want to only allow an IAM user

137
00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:00.420
to create an EBS volume if it has the Env

138
00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:02.880
and the CostCenter tags,

139
00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.220
then the policy would look like this.

140
00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:07.860
So we have a match all keys, okay?

141
00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:10.309
So ForAllValues:StringEquals,

142
00:06:10.309 --> 00:06:12.150
TagKeys, Env, and CostCenter,

143
00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:13.980
and this means that we want absolutely

144
00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:18.030
both the Env and the CostCenter tags to be present

145
00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:21.630
in the EBS volume when we create it.

146
00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:25.709
And you have an option to also use the value called

147
00:06:25.709 --> 00:06:28.110
ForAnyValue, which means that at a minimum,

148
00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:30.150
you must have either of these tags.

149
00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:33.360
So, on the second example here in this slide,

150
00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:37.140
here, if the EBS volume is created with the Env

151
00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.960
but not the CostCenter, it's going to work, and vice versa

152
00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:43.530
because we have the ForAnyValue condition key, okay?

153
00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:45.000
So this can come up in exam,

154
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.760
using the AWS Tag Keys condition key.

155
00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.840
Another example of SCP you can have is an SCP

156
00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:54.000
to deny an entire region using the condition

157
00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.145
named aws:RequestRegion.

158
00:06:57.145 --> 00:06:59.970
So the idea is that you would specify a Deny effect,

159
00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:01.650
and then you would say that the region

160
00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:04.440
where everything is denied could be eu-central-1

161
00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:05.850
and eu-west-1.

162
00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.070
You can specify different AWS services

163
00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:12.030
by specifying action, EC2 star, RDS star, and so on.

164
00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:13.770
And you can also have a bypass.

165
00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:16.440
For example, you can have ArnNotLike,

166
00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:19.170
so that two roles, for example, are allowed

167
00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:21.495
to bypass this SCP.

168
00:07:21.495 --> 00:07:22.328
So it can get quite complex,

169
00:07:22.328 --> 00:07:24.570
but the idea out of it is that SCP can be used

170
00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:27.390
to restrict member accounts to specific regions

171
00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.313
or from specific regions.

172
00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:33.210
Okay, so there's also a way of using SCPs

173
00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:35.400
to restrict creating resources

174
00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:37.950
that don't have the appropriate tags, okay?

175
00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:40.440
So, this is to prevent IAM users and roles

176
00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.210
for performing stuff that they can't do.

177
00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:45.600
For example, we can restrict launching an EC2 instance

178
00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:49.170
if it does not have the Project and CostCenter tags.

179
00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:52.890
So in this example, where the condition in your SCP

180
00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:57.890
is going to be a null for aws:RequestTag/Project, true.

181
00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:00.990
Okay, that means that you're going to, and this is a deny,

182
00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:03.750
So that means that it's going to deny any request

183
00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:08.750
that does not contain the Project tag in our EBS volume.

184
00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:11.880
And then in the second one, it is CostCenter as well, okay?

185
00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:15.270
So the first example was using IAM policies directly

186
00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:17.010
to say, yes, please use these tags.

187
00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:18.990
And in the second example that I showed you,

188
00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:20.763
this is for SCPs only.

189
00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:23.220
Next we have TAG policies.

190
00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:27.420
So, TAG policies are defined at the organization level,

191
00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:28.740
and they help you standardize tags

192
00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:31.290
across all resources in an organization.

193
00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:33.450
So this is used to ensure consistent tags,

194
00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:34.680
audit tag resources,

195
00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:37.560
maintain proper resource categorization, and so on.

196
00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:41.310
So you define tag keys and their allowed values,

197
00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:44.220
and this helps with Cost Allocation Tags,

198
00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:47.760
or if you wanted to do Attributes Access Control.

199
00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.550
And then any non-compliant tagging operations

200
00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:53.610
will be prevented, okay, on these specified services

201
00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:54.870
and resources.

202
00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:58.140
And you can also have a report that lists all the tags

203
00:08:58.140 --> 00:08:59.580
or non-compliant resources.

204
00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:01.980
And then if you want to look for non-compliant tags,

205
00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:03.840
then use Amazon EventBridge.

206
00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.000
So in this example, on the right hand side,

207
00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:07.920
for the Secrets Manager service,

208
00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.350
we can only have the key, CostCenter,

209
00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:12.873
to have the value 100 or 200.

210
00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:16.500
Another type of policy on organizations

211
00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:19.350
is called the AI Services Opt-out Policy.

212
00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.960
So, the backtrack of the context of this is

213
00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:26.070
that while the AI services in AWS may use your content

214
00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:30.330
that you're actually giving them to improve the Amazon AI

215
00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:32.610
or machine learning services.

216
00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:35.880
So for example, Amazon Lex, Amazon Comprehend, Amazon Polly,

217
00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:38.310
all of these services improve

218
00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:39.750
based on the data you give them.

219
00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:42.870
And so your data is used by AWS

220
00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:44.670
for improving the services,

221
00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:48.120
but you can opt out of having the content stored,

222
00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:50.370
used by AI services for improvements.

223
00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:52.980
And for this, you have to create an Opt-out policy,

224
00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:56.700
which will apply to all member accounts and all AWS regions.

225
00:09:56.700 --> 00:09:59.670
So you can opt out of all AI services using this,

226
00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:03.810
or you can opt out just for specific AWS services.

227
00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:05.370
For example, on the bottom right,

228
00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:08.850
I'm opting out of Rekognition and Amazon Lex.

229
00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:10.620
These policies can be attached

230
00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:13.350
to a Organization Root, specific OUs,

231
00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:17.100
or directly in individual member accounts.

232
00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.140
And then we have backup policies.

233
00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:21.750
So backup policies help you define backup plans

234
00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:23.580
using AWS Backup.

235
00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:25.890
And so we define these backup policies

236
00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:29.130
at the organization level with JSON documents

237
00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:30.750
and is going to give you granular control

238
00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:32.490
over the backing up the resources,

239
00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:34.530
such as the frequency, the time window,

240
00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.510
the backup region, et cetera, et cetera.

241
00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:40.350
These can be attached to the Organization Root OU

242
00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:43.860
or specific OU or an individual member account.

243
00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:46.170
It's an immutable backup plan,

244
00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:48.060
so they will appear in the member accounts

245
00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:52.260
within AWS Backups, but this is a view-only type of view

246
00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:55.710
that can only be managed within the management accounts

247
00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:57.390
of your organization.

248
00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:00.510
So that's it for all the policies on AWS organizations.

249
00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:03.510
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

